// VaadVivaad seed corpus — landmark Indian criminal judgments.
//
// THIS IS A SEED, NOT THE CORPUS. It exists so retrieval, reranking and
// citation verification have real ground truth to work against on a fresh
// install, and so the ingest pipeline can be exercised end to end.
//
// The holdings below are stated at the level of the well-established
// proposition each case is cited for. They are NOT substitutes for the
// judgments and carry no pin-cites. Before production use, replace this file
// with a real ingest from the free official publishers -- the Supreme Court
// judgment portal, eCourts, India Code and the Gazette (see
// app/services/ingest/pipeline.py) -- and have the entries checked.
//
// No commercial legal database is needed. Everything this project depends on
// is free or self-hosted, with Gemini as the single external service.
//
// `source: curated` marks these as citable. Anything with an untrusted source
// is refused by the pipeline and can never be offered to counsel.
{"citation_id": "sc:nanavati-1962", "case_name": "K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1961-11-24", "sections": ["300", "302"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "For Exception 1 to section 300 the provocation must be both grave and sudden, and the accused must have been deprived of self-control at the time of the act. Where sufficient time has elapsed for passion to subside and for reason to reassert itself, the exception is unavailable and the offence remains murder.", "summary": "Naval officer shot his wife's paramour after learning of the affair. The Supreme Court considered whether the killing fell within the grave and sudden provocation exception to murder, holding that the interval between the provocation and the act allowed for premeditation.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:bachan-singh-1980", "case_name": "Bachan Singh v. State of Punjab", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1980-05-09", "sections": ["302"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "The death sentence under section 302 is constitutional but may be imposed only in the rarest of rare cases where the alternative of life imprisonment is unquestionably foreclosed. Both the circumstances of the crime and the circumstances of the offender must be weighed, and reasons recorded.", "summary": "Constitution Bench challenge to the constitutional validity of the death penalty for murder. The Court upheld the provision while confining its use through the rarest of rare doctrine and requiring individualised sentencing.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:machhi-singh-1983", "case_name": "Machhi Singh v. State of Punjab", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1983-07-20", "sections": ["302"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "Elaborating Bachan Singh, the Court identified categories bearing on whether a case is rarest of rare, including the manner and motive of the killing, the magnitude of the crime and the personality of the victim, and directed that aggravating and mitigating circumstances be balanced before a death sentence is imposed.", "summary": "Series of killings arising from a family feud. The Court set out guidance for applying the rarest of rare test in practice.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04", "cites": [{"citation_id": "sc:bachan-singh-1980", "treatment": "followed"}]}
{"citation_id": "sc:sharad-sarda-1984", "case_name": "Sharad Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1984-07-17", "sections": ["302"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "A conviction resting on circumstantial evidence requires that the circumstances from which guilt is inferred be fully established; that they be consistent only with the hypothesis of guilt; that they be conclusive in nature and tendency; that they exclude every hypothesis except guilt; and that the chain of evidence be so complete as to leave no reasonable ground for a conclusion consistent with innocence.", "summary": "Death of a young woman by poisoning; the prosecution case rested wholly on circumstantial evidence. The Court restated the governing principles, commonly cited as the five golden principles or panchsheel of circumstantial evidence.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04", "cites": [{"citation_id": "sc:hanumant-1952", "treatment": "followed"}]}
{"citation_id": "sc:hanumant-1952", "case_name": "Hanumant v. State of Madhya Pradesh", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1952-05-27", "sections": ["302"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "In a case of circumstantial evidence the facts proved must be incompatible with the innocence of the accused and incapable of explanation on any other reasonable hypothesis. Suspicion, however strong, cannot take the place of proof.", "summary": "Early statement of the standard governing convictions founded on circumstantial evidence, repeatedly followed in later authority.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:gurmit-singh-1996", "case_name": "State of Punjab v. Gurmit Singh", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1996-01-16", "sections": ["376"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "The evidence of a prosecutrix in a case of sexual assault stands on a footing comparable to that of an injured witness, and a conviction may rest on her sole testimony where it inspires confidence. Corroboration is not a requirement of law, and minor contradictions or delay in lodging the complaint do not by themselves discredit the account.", "summary": "Appeal against acquittal in a case of rape of a schoolgirl. The Court criticised the trial court's approach to the prosecutrix's evidence and set out how such testimony is to be assessed.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:selvi-2010", "case_name": "Selvi v. State of Karnataka", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "2010-05-05", "sections": ["161", "162"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "Involuntary administration of narcoanalysis, polygraph examination and brain electrical activation profiling violates the right against self-incrimination under Article 20(3) and the right to personal liberty under Article 21. Results of such tests conducted without consent are inadmissible, though material subsequently discovered with the aid of voluntarily given information may be admissible.", "summary": "Challenge to the compulsory use of scientific interrogation techniques on accused persons, suspects and witnesses.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:lalita-kumari-2013", "case_name": "Lalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "2013-11-12", "sections": ["154"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "Registration of a first information report is mandatory under section 154 of the Code of Criminal Procedure where the information discloses a cognizable offence, and no preliminary inquiry is permissible in such a case. A preliminary inquiry is permitted only where it is not clear whether the information discloses a cognizable offence, and must be concluded within a limited period.", "summary": "Constitution Bench reference on whether police are bound to register an FIR on receiving information disclosing a cognizable offence.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:arnesh-kumar-2014", "case_name": "Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "2014-07-02", "sections": ["498A"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "Arrest is not automatic upon registration of a case for an offence punishable with imprisonment up to seven years. Police must satisfy themselves of the necessity of arrest against the parameters of section 41 of the Code of Criminal Procedure and record reasons, and magistrates must apply their mind before authorising detention.", "summary": "Directions issued to curb mechanical arrests, arising in the context of complaints under section 498A of the Indian Penal Code.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:dk-basu-1996", "case_name": "D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "1996-12-18", "sections": ["41", "50"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "The Court laid down binding requirements for arrest and detention, including accurate identification of the arresting officers, preparation of an arrest memo attested by a witness and countersigned by the arrestee, notification of a relative or friend, and periodic medical examination. Non-compliance renders the officer liable to departmental action and contempt.", "summary": "Public interest proceedings concerning custodial violence and deaths in police custody.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:pakala-narayana-1939", "case_name": "Pakala Narayana Swami v. Emperor", "court": "Privy Council", "date": "1939-01-19", "sections": ["302"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "A statement is a dying declaration where it relates to the cause of the declarant's death or the circumstances of the transaction resulting in death. The circumstances must have some proximate relation to the actual occurrence; a statement about distant events is not admissible on that footing.", "summary": "Leading authority on the scope of statements admissible as to the cause of death, followed in Indian courts on section 32(1) of the Evidence Act.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
{"citation_id": "sc:anvar-pv-2014", "case_name": "Anvar P.V. v. P.K. Basheer", "court": "Supreme Court of India", "date": "2014-09-18", "sections": ["65B"], "codes": ["IPC"], "holding": "Electronic records produced as evidence must be accompanied by a certificate satisfying the requirements of section 65B(4) of the Evidence Act. In the absence of such a certificate, secondary evidence of the contents of an electronic record is inadmissible, and oral evidence cannot cure the defect.", "summary": "Authority on the admissibility of electronic evidence including CCTV footage, call records and other computer output.", "source": "curated", "source_url": "", "retrieved_at": "2026-09-04"}
